Two declarations in libotr's own headers say () where they mean (void).

    src/instag.h        otrl_instag_t otrl_instag_get_new();
    src/context_priv.h  ConnContextPriv *otrl_context_priv_new();

In C an empty parameter list is not "no arguments", it is "unspecified", and clang
has warned about it under -Wstrict-prototypes for years. Adium compiles with that
warning on, and the headers travel in libotr.framework, so every source file that
reaches one of them collects the warning: ten of them in a Release build, from five
files that include <libotr/context.h>, which pulls both.

The framework committed in this tree does not have the problem, because somebody
edited the two lines in place after the framework was built. That edit is not in any
recipe, so the first freshly built libotr.framework brought the warnings back. This
patch is that edit, in a form the build applies by itself.

libotr 4.1.1 is from 2016 and has had no release since, so this is not going to be
fixed upstream and the patch is not going to stop applying.
